	abstract = {Rising labor costs, supply chain restructuring, and increasing protectionist trade policies have intensified the need for systematic cost reduction in electronics manufacturing. Facility layout and production-line configuration are critical factors influencing plant-level efficiency, yet existing optimization approaches face practical limitations in large-scale industrial environments: exact mathematical programming methods often require excessive computation time, while metaheuristic algorithms may exhibit convergence instability and inconsistent solution quality. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a hybrid optimization framework that integrates genetic algorithms with mixed-integer linear programming to determine cost-efficient facility-layout and production-line configuration decisions in electronics manufacturing plants. The genetic algorithm performs a global search over feasible line-configuration combinations, while the mixed-integer linear programming component evaluates each candidate solution by enforcing operational constraints and computing the associated manufacturing cost, enabling efficient exploration of large combinatorial solution spaces while maintaining feasibility and stability. Using an anonymized case study of an appliance manufacturer producing refrigerators, washing machines, and microwave ovens, we analyze the performance of the proposed model against i) the current facility layout, ii) five engineer-designed what-if scenarios, and iii) representative state-of-the-art optimization approaches under identical industrial constraints. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework consistently achieves lower manufacturing cost and more reliable convergence behavior than existing optimization approaches. These findings highlight the effectiveness of combining metaheuristic global search with mathematical programming-based feasibility enforcement for solving constrained industrial facility-layout problems.